484
00:29:05,956 --> 00:29:08,656
So you can see volatility. Volatility rises.

485
00:29:08,656 --> 00:29:11,256
Price bars are getting longer.

486
00:29:11,756 --> 00:29:13,856
You're getting those imbalances in the market.

487
00:29:13,856 --> 00:29:16,456
You're getting the heavier volume, getting the stronger delta.

488
00:29:17,056 --> 00:29:19,056
And then volatility decreases.

489
00:29:19,156 --> 00:29:26,356
Once the market sort of found liquidity, you can see how the bars got a lot smaller here, here, here.

490
00:29:26,656 --> 00:29:28,056
Volume is getting a lot smaller.

491
00:29:28,256 --> 00:29:31,756
So volatility rises. Volatility decreases.

492
00:29:34,956 --> 00:29:40,056
So to further understand volatility, traders can examine the price locations associated with its occurrence.

493
00:29:40,056 --> 00:29:46,056
By observing price levels, it becomes evident that volatility can manifest at both lower and higher price points.

494
00:29:46,156 --> 00:29:49,356
You know, people think that market's volatile, it's going to go higher.

495
00:29:49,356 --> 00:29:51,456
No, it can also go down.

496
00:29:51,856 --> 00:29:57,856
So furthermore, it's essential to identify where volatility is predominantly driven by sellers or buyers, right?

